# Normalize line endings so a file edited on Windows does not come back as a
# whole-file rewrite on Linux (and vice versa). Without this, a CRLF checkout
# of a Python file produces a diff touching every line, which is how a
# three-line bug fix ends up buried under 1160 changed lines.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect text vs binary; `eol=lf` makes the working tree
# LF everywhere. Blob normalization happens on checkin, so files already
# committed with CRLF convert naturally as they are edited rather than all at
# once.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows scripts genuinely need CRLF to execute.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary assets: never touched.
#
# `-text` alone is not enough here. The `* text=auto eol=lf` rule above also
# sets `eol` on these paths, and per gitattributes(5) `eol` "enables
# end-of-line conversion without any further checks" — it does not defer to
# `text`. Leaving `eol` set made git run the CRLF clean filter over JPEG,
# PNG, TTF and WOFF2 bytes on every status check, so 300+ binaries reported
# as modified on a clean checkout (the files were byte-identical to their
# blobs; only the comparison disagreed). Unsetting both keeps the `*` rule
# from reaching them.
*.jpg -text -eol
*.jpeg -text -eol
*.png -text -eol
*.gif -text -eol
*.ico -text -eol
*.webp -text -eol
*.pdf -text -eol
*.ttf -text -eol
*.otf -text -eol
*.woff -text -eol
*.woff2 -text -eol
*.mp3 -text -eol
*.mp4 -text -eol
*.wav -text -eol
*.zip -text -eol
*.jar -text -eol
*.keystore -text -eol
*.jks -text -eol
